//? Title: The Bellman–Ford Algorithm
//? Subtitle: Shortest paths with negative weights as a dynamic program over path length, negative cycles as perpetual-motion loops, and a potential-function check, on an e-bike energy map of Maunga Whau with datamunge's algorithms::bellman_ford
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Bellman–Ford computes shortest paths when edge weights may be negative, and detects the case
where none exist because a negative cycle is reachable. We derive it as a dynamic program over
the number of edges in a path, prove that \(n-1\) passes suffice without a negative cycle and
that one more pass exposes one, and compare the pass form with the queue form. The data is an
e-bike energy map of Maunga Whau. Climbing costs battery energy and regenerative braking
recovers some of it on the way down, so 38% of the 41,572 steps have negative cost. A loop
argument shows there is no negative cycle unless the recovery exceeds the climbing cost, which
would be a perpetual-motion machine. The same argument gives a potential that lets Dijkstra's
algorithm check every distance, and the two agree to \(4\times10^{-12}\). Raising the credit
just past the climbing cost produces a negative cycle at once, a two-cell loop on the steepest
slope, and `bellman_ford` finds and returns it.
)

\toc

> Shortest paths when weights can be negative

Take a directed graph with \(n\) vertices and weighted edges \((u,v,w)\), where \(w\) may be
negative. The distance \(\delta(s,v)\) from a source \(s\) is the least total weight of a path from
\(s\) to \(v\). Negative weights change two things.

First, greedy label setting breaks. Dijkstra's algorithm finalises the closest unsettled vertex,
on the grounds that any other route must pass through a vertex at least as far away and can
only get longer. A negative edge further on can make it shorter, so a settled distance may be
wrong. (`algorithms::dijkstra` refuses negative weights for this reason.)

Second, a shortest path may not exist. If a cycle reachable from \(s\) has negative total weight,
going round it once more always lowers the cost, and every vertex reachable from the cycle has
\(\delta=-\infty\). An algorithm must detect this case rather than return numbers.

>> A dynamic program over path length

Let \(D_k(v)\) be the least weight of a walk from \(s\) to \(v\) with at most \(k\) edges, with
\(D_0(s)=0\) and \(D_0(v)=\infty\) otherwise. The last edge of such a walk enters \(v\) from some
\(u\), so

$$
D_k(v)=\min\Big(D_{k-1}(v),\;\min_{(u,v,w)}\big(D_{k-1}(u)+w\big)\Big).
$$
\alttext(D k of v is the minimum of D k minus 1 of v and, over edges u v w, D k minus 1 of u plus w.)

Bellman–Ford (Bellman 1958, Ford 1956, Moore 1959) evaluates this recurrence in place. One
*pass* relaxes every edge, \(d(v)\leftarrow\min(d(v),d(u)+w)\). Doing it in place can only help,
so after \(k\) passes \(d(v)\le D_k(v)\). Every value of \(d\) is also the weight of a real walk,
so \(d(v)\ge\delta(s,v)\).

*No negative cycle.* Removing a cycle from a walk then never increases its weight, so some
shortest walk is a simple path, with at most \(n-1\) edges. Hence
\(\delta(s,v)\le d(v)\le D_{n-1}(v)=\delta(s,v)\): after \(n-1\) passes every distance is exact. More
precisely, if every shortest path can be chosen with at most \(k\) edges, \(k\) passes suffice,
and pass \(k+1\) changes nothing. The algorithm stops at the first pass that changes nothing.

*A negative cycle.* Suppose the cycle \(v_0,v_1,\dots,v_{m}=v_0\) is reachable, and no edge could
be relaxed any more. Then \(d(v_{i})\le d(v_{i-1})+w(v_{i-1},v_i)\) for every \(i\), and summing
round the cycle,

$$
\sum_{i=1}^{m}d(v_i)\le\sum_{i=1}^{m}d(v_{i-1})+\sum_{i=1}^{m}w(v_{i-1},v_i)
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad 0\le\sum_{i=1}^{m}w(v_{i-1},v_i)<0,
$$
\alttext(The sum of d of v i is at most the sum of d of v i minus 1 plus the sum of the weights round the cycle; the two sums of d are equal, so 0 is at most the cycle weight, which is negative, a contradiction.)

because both sums of \(d\) run over the same vertices and are finite. This is a contradiction, so
some edge can always be relaxed, and the \(n\)-th pass will find one. Following the
predecessor pointers back \(n\) steps from the vertex it relaxes lands on the cycle, which
`bellman_ford` returns as `negative_cycle`.

The cost is \(O(nE)\) in the worst case. `algorithms::bellman_ford_queue` is the queue form (Moore
1959, often called SPFA). Each round examines only the out-edges of vertices whose distance fell
in the previous round. It computes the same \(D_k\) and usually does far less work. It detects a
negative cycle when some predecessor chain reaches \(n\) edges.

> An energy map of Maunga Whau

Negative weights arise wherever moving can *earn* something. An e-bike with regenerative braking
spends battery energy riding and climbing, and recovers some of it on descents.
`dm::datasets::volcano()` is the topography of Maunga Whau, Auckland, on an \(87\times61\) grid of
10 m cells. We measure energy in *flat-metre equivalents*, the energy to ride one metre on the
flat, roughly 12 J for a 100 kg rider and bike. A step to one of the 8 neighbours costs

$$
c(u,v)=d(u,v)+\lambda\max(0,\Delta z)-\kappa\max(0,-\Delta z),\qquad \Delta z=z_v-z_u,
$$
\alttext(c of u v equals d of u v plus lambda times the height gained minus kappa times the height lost, where delta z is z v minus z u.)

with \(d\) the horizontal length. Lifting 100 kg by one metre takes 0.98 kJ, about 1.2 kJ of
battery energy, so \(\lambda=100\). The credit \(\kappa\) is the energy recovered per metre of
descent; regenerating about half of it gives \(\kappa=0.5\lambda=50\).
`algorithms::terrain_energy_edges` builds these 41,572 directed edges. Any step that drops more
than \(d/\kappa\), 0.2 m per 10 m, has negative cost.

>> Why there is no negative cycle, and a check on the answer

Around any closed loop, the height gained equals the height lost, \(A\). So a loop of length
\(L\) costs

$$
c(\text{loop})=L+\lambda A-\kappa A=L+(\lambda-\kappa)A>0\qquad\text{when }\kappa\le\lambda.
$$
\alttext(The cost of a loop is L plus lambda A minus kappa A, which equals L plus lambda minus kappa times A, positive when kappa is at most lambda.)

Energy recovered on a descent can never exceed what the climb cost, so there are no negative
cycles. A credit \(\kappa>\lambda\) would be a perpetual-motion machine, and then a steep enough
up-and-down loop has \(L<(\kappa-\lambda)A\) and negative cost.

The same observation gives an independent check. With the *potential* \(p(v)=\kappa z_v\), the
reweighted cost \(c'(u,v)=c(u,v)+p(u)-p(v)\) is

$$
c'(u,v)=\begin{cases}d(u,v)+(\lambda-\kappa)\Delta z,&\Delta z>0,\\ d(u,v),&\Delta z\le0,\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(c prime of u v is d of u v plus lambda minus kappa times delta z when climbing, and just d of u v otherwise.)

which is never negative when \(\kappa\le\lambda\). The potentials cancel along any path, so
\(\delta(s,v)=\delta'(s,v)-\kappa(z_s-z_v)\), and \(\delta'\) can be computed by Dijkstra's algorithm.
This is Johnson's reweighting (Johnson 1977), with the potential handed to us by physics instead
of found by a Bellman–Ford run.

>> Riding down from the summit

We start at the summit, so most cells are downhill and many distances are negative: the
battery holds more on arrival than at the start.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bellman_ford_energy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const double lambda = 100.0, kappa = 50.0;
  const auto grid = alg::terrain_from_frame(dm::datasets::volcano(), "x", "y", "elevation", lambda);
  const std::size_t n = grid.elevation.size();
  const std::size_t summit = std::ranges::max_element(grid.elevation) - grid.elevation.begin();
  const auto edges = alg::terrain_energy_edges(grid, kappa);
  const auto negative = std::ranges::count_if(edges, [](const auto& e) { return std::get<2>(e) < 0; });

  const auto time_ms = [](auto&& f) {
    const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    auto r = f();
    return std::pair{r, std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count()};
  };
  const auto [bf, bf_ms] = time_ms([&] { return alg::bellman_ford(n, edges, summit); });
  const auto [queue, queue_ms] = time_ms([&] { return alg::bellman_ford_queue(n, edges, summit); });

  // Johnson check: Dijkstra on the reweighted costs, then undo the potential.
  auto reduced = edges;
  for (auto& [u, v, w] : reduced) w = std::max(0.0, w + kappa * (grid.elevation[u] - grid.elevation[v]));
  const auto [dj, dj_ms] = time_ms([&] { return alg::dijkstra(n, reduced, summit); });
  double worst = 0.0, worst_queue = 0.0;
  std::size_t below_zero = 0, deepest = 0;
  for (std::size_t v = 0; v < n; ++v) {
    const double johnson = dj.distance[v] - kappa * (grid.elevation[summit] - grid.elevation[v]);
    worst = std::max(worst, std::abs(bf.distance[v] - johnson));
    worst_queue = std::max(worst_queue, std::abs(bf.distance[v] - queue.distance[v]));
    below_zero += bf.distance[v] < 0;
    deepest = std::max(deepest, alg::shortest_path_to(bf, v).size() - 1);
  }

  std::print("Summit at ({:.0f} m, {:.0f} m), {:.0f} m high. {} cells, {} directed steps, {} with negative cost ({:.0f}%).\n\n",
             grid.cell_size * (summit / grid.cols), grid.cell_size * (summit % grid.cols), grid.elevation[summit], n,
             edges.size(), negative, 100.0 * negative / edges.size());
  std::print("| method | rounds | edges examined | negative cycle | time (ms) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| Bellman–Ford, passes | {} | {} | {} | {:.2f} |\n", bf.passes, bf.edge_checks,
             bf.has_negative_cycle ? "yes" : "no", bf_ms);
  std::print("| Bellman–Ford, queue | {} | {} | {} | {:.2f} |\n", queue.passes, queue.edge_checks,
             queue.has_negative_cycle ? "yes" : "no", queue_ms);
  std::print("| Dijkstra on reweighted costs | – | {} | – | {:.2f} |\n\n", reduced.size(), dj_ms);
  std::print("Largest difference from the Johnson check: {:.1e} (queue form: {:.1e}). The deepest shortest path has {} "
             "steps; the bound n − 1 is {}. {} cells ({:.0f}%) end with more energy than at the summit.\n",
             worst, worst_queue, deepest, n - 1, below_zero, 100.0 * below_zero / n);

  // Left: energy to reach each cell, with three shortest paths. Right: relaxations per round.
  std::vector<double> xs, ys;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < grid.rows; ++i) xs.push_back(grid.cell_size * i);
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < grid.cols; ++j) ys.push_back(grid.cell_size * j);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(grid.rows, std::vector<double>(grid.cols));
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < n; ++c) z[c / grid.cols][c % grid.cols] = bf.distance[c];
  const double extent = std::abs(std::ranges::max(bf.distance, {}, [](double d) { return std::abs(d); }));
  std::vector<dm::RGB> diverging = {{33, 102, 172}, {67, 147, 195}, {146, 197, 222}, {209, 229, 240},
                                    {247, 247, 247}, {253, 219, 199}, {244, 165, 130}, {214, 96, 77},
                                    {178, 24, 43}};
  auto map = dm::graphics::RPlot::create().image(xs, ys, z, diverging, -extent, extent);
  for (const std::size_t target : {std::size_t{80 * 61 + 5}, std::size_t{5 * 61 + 55}, std::size_t{86 * 61 + 60}}) {
    std::vector<double> px, py;
    for (const std::size_t c : alg::shortest_path_to(bf, target)) {
      px.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c / grid.cols));
      py.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c % grid.cols));
    }
    map.lines(px, py, "", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 2.0).points({px.back()}, {py.back()}, "", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 6.0);
  }
  map.points({grid.cell_size * (summit / grid.cols)}, {grid.cell_size * (summit % grid.cols)}, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 9.0)
      .title(std::format("Energy from the summit (blue < 0 < red, ±{:.0f})", extent))
      .x_label("x (m)")
      .y_label("y (m)");
  const auto rounds = [](const std::vector<std::size_t>& counts) {
    std::pair<std::vector<double>, std::vector<double>> xy;
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < counts.size(); ++k) {
      xy.first.push_back(static_cast<double>(k + 1));
      xy.second.push_back(std::log10(std::max<std::size_t>(counts[k], 1)));
    }
    return xy;
  };
  const auto [kx, ky] = rounds(bf.relaxations_per_pass);
  const auto [qx, qy] = rounds(queue.relaxations_per_pass);
  auto work = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                  .lines(kx, ky, "passes over all edges", dm::RGB{217, 119, 6}, 2.0)
                  .points(kx, ky, "", dm::RGB{217, 119, 6}, 5.0)
                  .lines(qx, qy, "queue rounds", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
                  .y_limits(-0.1, 4.6)
                  .title("Distances lowered in each round")
                  .x_label("round")
                  .y_label("log10 relaxations (0 plotted at 0)");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(map).add(work).size(1250, 480).save_svg("build/bellman_ford_energy.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Summit at (190 m, 300 m), 195 m high. 5307 cells, 41572 directed steps, 15934 with negative cost (38%).

| method | rounds | edges examined | negative cycle | time (ms) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: |
| Bellman–Ford, passes | 31 | 1288732 | no | 0.98 |
| Bellman–Ford, queue | 68 | 42750 | no | 0.67 |
| Dijkstra on reweighted costs | – | 41572 | – | 0.93 |

Largest difference from the Johnson check: 3.6e-12 (queue form: 0.0e+00). The deepest shortest path has 73 steps; the bound n − 1 is 5306. 5306 cells (100%) end with more energy than at the summit.
\image(figures/bellman_ford_energy.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: the energy, in flat-metre equivalents, needed to ride from the summit (red dot) to every cell, blue where the battery gains and red where it loses, with the shortest routes to three corners. Right: the number of distances lowered in each round, for passes over the whole edge list and for the queue form.)
\alttext(Left: a map of the 860 by 600 metre area in shades of blue, near white on the summit plateau around x equals 190 to 300, y equals 250 to 400, and darkening outwards in rings that follow the contours of the volcano, darkest at the map edges. A red dot marks the summit at (190, 300). Three black routes run from it: one north-west to (50, 550), one east along y equals 450 to the top-right corner, and one south-east along y equals 200 then down to (800, 50). Right: log10 relaxations per round. The orange pass series starts at 3.75 in round 1, drops to 2.2 in round 2, rises slowly to 2.5 around round 18 and falls to 0 at round 31. The blue queue series starts below 1, rises to about 2.4 by round 30, stays near 2 until round 67, and drops to 0 at round 68.)

The summit is 195 m high. With \(\kappa=50\), every downhill step earns energy, so every other
cell is reached with more energy than at the start, up to 4,473 flat-metre equivalents (about
54 kJ) at the foot of the hill. The map is close to a contour map, because most of the energy is
the potential term \(\kappa(z_s-z_v)\); the horizontal distance adds only a little. The three
routes run straight down the slopes, and none of them climbs at all.

The pass form converged after 30 passes and confirmed it with a 31st that changed nothing,
far fewer than the bound \(n-1=5{,}306\). The deepest shortest path has 73 steps, so the
\(D_k\) argument alone would allow up to 74 passes. Fewer were needed because relaxing in place
lets a pass carry a distance along several edges when they come in the right order: the first
pass alone lowered over 5,000 distances. The queue form needs 68 rounds, since it moves
exactly one edge per round, but it examines only 42,750 edges, against 1.29 million for the
passes, a thirtieth of the work.

The Johnson check, Dijkstra's algorithm on the reweighted costs \(c'\), agrees with Bellman–Ford
to within \(4\times10^{-12}\) at every cell, and the queue form agrees exactly. All three take
under a millisecond on this machine.

> Turning up the credit

We now vary the credit \(\kappa\) from 0 to \(1.5\lambda\) and run Bellman–Ford from the summit each
time. Beyond \(\kappa=\lambda\) the loop argument above predicts negative cycles.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bellman_ford_credit.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const double lambda = 100.0;
  const auto grid = alg::terrain_from_frame(dm::datasets::volcano(), "x", "y", "elevation", lambda);
  const std::size_t n = grid.elevation.size();
  const std::size_t summit = std::ranges::max_element(grid.elevation) - grid.elevation.begin();
  const std::size_t foot = 5 * grid.cols + 55;  // the north-west foot

  std::print("| \\(\\kappa/\\lambda\\) | negative steps | negative cycle | passes | queue edges examined | "
             "energy to the NW foot | cells below 0 |\n| ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> ratio, to_foot;
  std::vector<std::size_t> cycle;
  double cycle_ratio = 0.0;
  for (const double r : {0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 0.9, 1.0, 1.05, 1.1, 1.25, 1.5}) {
    const auto edges = alg::terrain_energy_edges(grid, r * lambda);
    const auto negative = std::ranges::count_if(edges, [](const auto& e) { return std::get<2>(e) < 0; });
    const auto bf = alg::bellman_ford(n, edges, summit);
    const auto queue = alg::bellman_ford_queue(n, edges, summit);
    if (bf.has_negative_cycle) {
      std::print("| {:.2f} | {} | {} cells | {} | {} | – | – |\n", r, negative, bf.negative_cycle.size(), bf.passes,
                 queue.edge_checks);
      if (cycle.empty()) {
        cycle = bf.negative_cycle;
        cycle_ratio = r;
      }
      continue;
    }
    const auto below = std::ranges::count_if(bf.distance, [](double d) { return d < 0; });
    std::print("| {:.2f} | {} | no | {} | {} | {:.0f} | {} |\n", r, negative, bf.passes, queue.edge_checks,
               bf.distance[foot], below);
    ratio.push_back(r);
    to_foot.push_back(bf.distance[foot]);
  }

  // Describe the first negative cycle found.
  double length = 0.0, ascent = 0.0, cost = 0.0;
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < cycle.size(); ++k) {
    const std::size_t a = cycle[k], b = cycle[(k + 1) % cycle.size()];
    length += alg::terrain_step_cost(alg::TerrainGrid{grid.rows, grid.cols, grid.elevation, grid.cell_size, 0.0}, a, b);
    ascent += std::max(0.0, grid.elevation[b] - grid.elevation[a]);
  }
  cost = length + (lambda - cycle_ratio * lambda) * ascent;
  std::print("\nFirst negative cycle (κ/λ = {:.2f}): {} cells, length {:.1f} m, climb {:.0f} m, cost L + (λ − κ)A = {:.1f}.\n",
             cycle_ratio, cycle.size(), length, ascent, cost);

  std::vector<double> xs, ys, cx, cy;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < grid.rows; ++i) xs.push_back(grid.cell_size * i);
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < grid.cols; ++j) ys.push_back(grid.cell_size * j);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(grid.rows, std::vector<double>(grid.cols));
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < n; ++c) z[c / grid.cols][c % grid.cols] = grid.elevation[c];
  for (const std::size_t c : cycle) {
    cx.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c / grid.cols));
    cy.push_back(grid.cell_size * (c % grid.cols));
  }
  cx.push_back(cx.front());
  cy.push_back(cy.front());
  auto map = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .image(xs, ys, z, dm::graphics::terrain_colors(20))
                 .lines(cx, cy, "", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 3.0)
                 .points(cx, cy, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 9.0)
                 .title(std::format("A negative cycle at κ = {:.2f}λ", cycle_ratio))
                 .x_label("x (m)")
                 .y_label("y (m)");
  auto curve = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                   .lines(ratio, to_foot, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
                   .points(ratio, to_foot, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
                   .abline_v(1.0, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.0)
                   .title("Energy from the summit to the NW foot")
                   .x_label("credit κ / λ")
                   .y_label("flat-metre equivalents");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(curve).add(map).size(1200, 460).save_svg("build/bellman_ford_credit.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| \(\kappa/\lambda\) | negative steps | negative cycle | passes | queue edges examined | energy to the NW foot | cells below 0 |
| ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.00 | 0 | no | 31 | 42804 | 308 | 0 |
| 0.25 | 15934 | no | 31 | 42780 | -1867 | 5274 |
| 0.50 | 15934 | no | 31 | 42750 | -4042 | 5306 |
| 0.75 | 15934 | no | 31 | 42532 | -6217 | 5306 |
| 0.90 | 15934 | no | 31 | 42108 | -7522 | 5306 |
| 1.00 | 15934 | no | 31 | 41572 | -8392 | 5306 |
| 1.05 | 15934 | 2 cells | 5306 | 11646582 | – | – |
| 1.10 | 15934 | 2 cells | 5306 | 15400781 | – | – |
| 1.25 | 15934 | 2 cells | 5306 | 15352576 | – | – |
| 1.50 | 15934 | 2 cells | 5306 | 14230132 | – | – |

First negative cycle (κ/λ = 1.05): 2 cells, length 28.3 m, climb 8 m, cost L + (λ − κ)A = -11.7.
\image(figures/bellman_ford_credit.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: the energy needed to ride from the summit to the north-west foot of the map, for credits up to \(\lambda\); the red line marks \(\kappa=\lambda\). Right: the first negative cycle found once \(\kappa>\lambda\), drawn in red on the elevation map.)
\alttext(Left: a straight line falling from 308 at a credit ratio of 0 to minus 8,392 at a ratio of 1, through points at 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 and 0.9, with a red vertical line at 1. Right: the elevation map with two adjacent red dots joined by a short black segment at about x equals 375, y equals 505, on the steep north-eastern slope of the cone.)

The number of negative steps is the same, 15,934, for every credit from \(0.25\lambda\) up. The
elevations are recorded in whole metres, so every descending step drops at least 1 m, and a
credit of 25 per metre already outweighs the 10 m or 14.1 m of horizontal riding.

Up to \(\kappa=\lambda\) there is no negative cycle, the distances are finite and the pass count
stays at 31. The energy to the north-west foot falls exactly linearly, from 308 to \(-8{,}392\),
with slope \(-87\lambda\). The best route never climbs, so its cost is its length, 308 m, minus
\(\kappa\) times the 87 m drop from the summit to the foot.

At \(\kappa=1.05\lambda\), five percent more than perpetual motion allows, Bellman–Ford finds a
negative cycle at once. Every distance keeps falling, the algorithm runs all 5,306 passes, and
the extra pass returns a two-cell loop: one diagonal step down an 8 m drop and back up. It
costs \(L+(\lambda-\kappa)A=28.3-5\times8=-11.7\), exactly as the loop formula predicts. Every
larger credit also gives a two-cell cycle. The queue form detects the cycle too, but only after
examining 11.6 million or more edges, as distances spiral down until some predecessor chain is
\(n\) edges long.

> Summary

- Bellman–Ford evaluates the recurrence \(D_k(v)=\min(D_{k-1}(v),\min_u D_{k-1}(u)+w)\) in place;
  without a negative cycle \(n-1\) passes are exact, and a summing argument shows a reachable
  negative cycle always leaves an edge to relax.
- On the e-bike energy map of Maunga Whau, 38% of the steps are negative, yet a loop costs
  \(L+(\lambda-\kappa)A\), so there is no negative cycle while recovery is below the climbing cost.
- The potential \(\kappa z\) makes every reweighted cost non-negative, and Dijkstra's algorithm on
  the reweighted graph agrees with Bellman–Ford to \(4\times10^{-12}\).
- Only 31 of the 5,306 allowed passes were needed. The queue form (`bellman_ford_queue`) examined
  a thirtieth as many edges.
- At \(\kappa=1.05\lambda\) `bellman_ford` reports a negative cycle and returns it: a two-cell
  up-and-down loop costing \(-11.7\), a perpetual-motion machine on the steepest slope.

> References

